@export and the Inspector:
```gdscript
@export var speed := 220.0
@export var max_health := 100
@export var sprite_texture: Texture2D
@export var target: Node2D
@export var loot: Array[Resource] = []
```

Every one of these appears in the Inspector, is **saved into the `.tscn`**, and can differ per instance. This is the bridge between code and editor and it is the single most important habit in Godot: a number you tune belongs in an `@export`, not in the script body.

Hints control the widget:

```gdscript
@export_range(0, 500, 10) var speed := 220.0
@export_enum("Melee", "Ranged", "Magic") var kind := 0
@export_multiline var description := ""
@export_file("*.tscn") var next_level: String
@export_color_no_alpha var tint := Color.WHITE
@export_node_path("Node2D") var follow: NodePath
```

And for organisation, which matters once a script has twenty exports:

```gdscript
@export_group("Movement")
@export var speed := 220.0
@export var accel := 900.0
@export_group("Combat")
@export var damage := 7
```

`@export_group`, `@export_subgroup` and `@export_category` fold the Inspector into readable sections. An exported variable's **default comes from the script**; changing it in code does not change instances that already overrode it.

Velocity, acceleration, and making movem:
Setting `velocity = dir * speed` is instant start and instant stop. It reads as rigid. The fix is acceleration:

```gdscript
@export var speed := 220.0
@export var accel := 1600.0
@export var friction := 1200.0

func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:
    var dir := Input.get_vector("left", "right", "up", "down")
    if dir != Vector2.ZERO:
        velocity = velocity.move_toward(dir * speed, accel * delta)
    else:
        velocity = velocity.move_toward(Vector2.ZERO, friction * delta)
    move_and_slide()
```

`move_toward` is the right tool: linear, frame-rate independent, and it never overshoots.

Gravity for a platformer, read from the project setting so it is in one place:

```gdscript
var gravity: float = ProjectSettings.get_setting("physics/2d/default_gravity")

if not is_on_floor():
    velocity.y += gravity * delta
if Input.is_action_just_pressed("jump") and is_on_floor():
    velocity.y = jump_velocity        # negative, because +Y is down
```

Three numbers that do most of the work on feel, all exported so you can tune them while running: acceleration, friction, and a shorter rise than fall (multiply gravity when `velocity.y > 0`).

RayCast2D, shape queries, and asking the:
A `RayCast2D` node casts every physics frame and caches the result:

```gdscript
if ray.is_colliding():
    var what := ray.get_collider()
    var point := ray.get_collision_point()
    var normal := ray.get_collision_normal()
```

`target_position` is the ray's end **in local space**, so it rotates with the node — which is exactly what you want for a line of sight on a turning character. `enabled`, `exclude_parent`, `collide_with_areas`, `collide_with_bodies`, and `add_exception(node)` are the properties worth knowing. `force_raycast_update()` casts immediately instead of waiting for the tick.

`ShapeCast2D` is the same idea with a shape instead of a line — the right tool for "will this fit" and for thick projectiles.

And when you need a one-off query with no node at all, go direct to the physics server:

```gdscript
var params := PhysicsRayQueryParameters2D.create(from, to)
params.collision_mask = 0b0010
var hit := get_world_2d().direct_space_state.intersect_ray(params)
if hit:
    print(hit.collider, hit.position)
```

`intersect_ray`, `intersect_point`, `intersect_shape`, `cast_motion`. These must be called from `_physics_process` — the space is locked during the rest of the frame and will error.

Resource: a custom data class with class:
A `Resource` is a data object that Godot can **save to a file, share between scenes, and edit in the Inspector**. It is the answer to "where do the numbers live".

```gdscript
class_name StatBlock
extends Resource

@export var display_name := ""
@export var max_health := 100
@export var speed := 220.0
@export var sprite_frames: SpriteFrames
@export var abilities: Array[AbilityData] = []
```

Then a node that uses it:

```gdscript
class_name Enemy
extends CharacterBody2D

@export var stats: StatBlock
```

The Inspector now shows a **StatBlock** slot on every Enemy. Drop a different `.tres` in and you have a different enemy with no new script and no new scene.

Why not a Dictionary: no typing, no autocomplete, no Inspector, and a typo'd key fails silently. Why not an inner class: cannot be saved, cannot be edited in the editor.

`_init()` on a Resource must have **all-default arguments**, because the editor constructs it with none. `Resource` also has `resource_name` and `resource_path`, and `changed` — a signal it emits when an exported property is edited, which `@tool` scripts can listen to.

@export hints, groups, and a readable In:
Beyond the basic types, the hints that make data authoring pleasant:

```gdscript
@export_range(0, 100, 1, "or_greater", "suffix:hp") var health := 100
@export_range(0.0, 1.0) var chance := 0.5
@export_enum("Melee:0", "Ranged:1", "Magic:2") var kind := 0
@export_flags("Fire:1", "Ice:2", "Poison:4") var immunities := 0
@export_multiline var description := ""
@export_file("*.tscn") var next_scene: String
@export_dir var art_folder: String
@export_color_no_alpha var tint := Color.WHITE
@export_node_path("Node2D", "Node3D") var follow: NodePath
@export_custom(PROPERTY_HINT_NONE, "") var raw
```

`@export_flags` gives a checkbox set backed by a bitmask, which is the right representation for damage types and tags. `"suffix:hp"` puts a unit in the spin box. `"or_greater"` lets you exceed the slider.

And the organisers:

```gdscript
@export_category("Archer")
@export_group("Combat", "combat_")   # second arg strips a name prefix
@export var combat_damage := 7
@export var combat_range := 180.0
@export_subgroup("Critical")
@export var combat_crit_chance := 0.05
```

A twenty-property resource with no groups is a resource nobody will author correctly. Ten minutes of grouping pays for itself the first time you fill in the fifth enemy.

`@export_storage` saves a value without showing it; `@export_tool_button` (4.4+) puts a button in the Inspector that calls a method.

FileAccess, user://, and saving a game:
`res://` is **read-only in an exported game** — it lives inside the package. Everything you write goes to `user://`, which is a real per-user directory (`~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/<project>/` on Linux).

```gdscript
const PATH := "user://save.dat"

func save_game(data: Dictionary) -> void:
    var f := FileAccess.open(PATH, FileAccess.WRITE)
    if f == null:
        push_error("save failed: %s" % FileAccess.get_open_error())
        return
    f.store_var(data, true)        # true = allow objects

func load_game() -> Dictionary:
    if not FileAccess.file_exists(PATH):
        return {}
    var f := FileAccess.open(PATH, FileAccess.READ)
    return f.get_var(true)
```

No `close()` needed — the file closes when the `FileAccess` goes out of scope, though `f = null` forces it.

Other methods: `store_string` / `get_as_text`, `store_line` / `get_line`, `store_8/16/32/64`, `get_buffer`, `seek`, `eof_reached`. `FileAccess.open_encrypted_with_pass()` if you care, which for a single-player game you probably should not.

`DirAccess` for directories: `make_dir_recursive`, `get_files`, `remove`, `rename`.

The habit that saves grief: **save a version number** in the dictionary from the very first version, and write the loader to handle an older one. A save format with no version is a save format you can never change.

Custom data layers, and reading them fro:
Per-tile data of your own — the thing that turns a tilemap from scenery into a system.

TileSet Inspector > **Custom Data Layers** > add one, name it (`footstep`, `speed_mult`, `is_water`, `damage`), choose its **Type** (String, int, float, bool, Vector2, Color…).

Then TileSet tab > Paint sub-tab > Property = your layer > select tiles > type the value. Or set it per tile in the Select sub-tab.

Reading it:

```gdscript
var cell := layer.local_to_map(layer.to_local(global_position))
var data := layer.get_cell_tile_data(cell)
if data:
    var sound: String = data.get_custom_data("footstep")
    var mult: float = data.get_custom_data("speed_mult")
    velocity *= mult
```

`get_cell_tile_data` returns `null` for an empty cell — always check it.

This is the clean answer to footstep sounds, slow mud, damaging lava, tiles the AI should avoid, and tiles that are diggable. The alternative — a parallel dictionary of coordinates, or a separate marker node per tile — is strictly worse: the data lives with the tile, is authored once, and cannot get out of sync with what is painted.

Also available per tile: `get_terrain()`, `get_navigation_polygon(layer)`, `z_index`, `y_sort_origin`, and the modulate/flip state.

NavigationAgent2D: target_position and g:
The agent does the pathfinding; **you still do the moving**. That split catches everyone.

```gdscript
@onready var agent: NavigationAgent2D = $NavigationAgent2D

func _ready() -> void:
    # The map is not ready on the first frame. Without this, the first
    # path request returns a straight line to the target.
    await get_tree().physics_frame
    agent.target_position = player.global_position

func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:
    if agent.is_navigation_finished():
        velocity = Vector2.ZERO
    else:
        var next := agent.get_next_path_position()
        velocity = global_position.direction_to(next) * speed
    move_and_slide()
```

Properties that matter: `path_desired_distance` and `target_desired_distance` (how close counts as arrived — too small and the agent jitters on the spot), `radius`, `navigation_layers`, `max_speed`.

Signals: `navigation_finished`, `target_reached`, `path_changed`, `waypoint_reached`, `link_reached`.

`agent.get_current_navigation_path()` returns the whole `PackedVector2Array`, which is what you draw to debug it.

Set `target_position` every frame if the target moves, but **re-path at an interval instead** — ten times a second is plenty, and a hundred agents re-pathing at 60 Hz is a measurable cost. A `Timer` on 0.1 seconds driving `agent.target_position = player.global_position` is the standard shape.

The canvas_item shader: vertex, fragment:
A shader is a small program the GPU runs per vertex and per pixel. In 2D you write `canvas_item` shaders. Create one: Inspector > `material` > **New ShaderMaterial** > its `shader` > **New Shader** > Shader Editor opens in the bottom panel.

```glsl
shader_type canvas_item;

void vertex() {
    VERTEX.y += sin(TIME * 2.0 + VERTEX.x * 0.1) * 3.0;
}

void fragment() {
    COLOR = texture(TEXTURE, UV);
    COLOR.rgb = mix(COLOR.rgb, vec3(1.0), 0.5);   // flash white
}

void light() {
    LIGHT = vec4(0.0);     // this item ignores 2D lights
}
```

The three entry points: `vertex()` moves geometry, `fragment()` decides colour, `light()` overrides how 2D lights affect it.

The built-ins you will use constantly: `COLOR` (the output, and on input the node's modulate), `TEXTURE` (the sprite), `UV` (0..1 across the sprite), `TIME` (seconds since start), `VERTEX`, `SCREEN_UV`, `TEXTURE_PIXEL_SIZE` (one pixel in UV units — how you sample a neighbour).

`render_mode unshaded;` after the shader_type skips lighting entirely. `render_mode blend_add;` for additive.

For `SCREEN_TEXTURE` you need `uniform sampler2D screen_tex : hint_screen_texture, repeat_disable, filter_nearest;` and in 2D a `BackBufferCopy` node covering the area — that is the part that is never obvious.

The language is GLSL-like: `vec2/3/4`, `float`, `mix`, `clamp`, `smoothstep`, `step`, `floor`, `fract`, `length`, `texture()`. No `if` is forbidden but branches cost more than arithmetic.

uniform, ShaderMaterial, and set_shader_:
A `uniform` is a shader variable you set from outside, and it is what makes a shader reusable:

```glsl
shader_type canvas_item;

uniform float flash : hint_range(0.0, 1.0) = 0.0;
uniform vec4 tint : source_color = vec4(1.0);
uniform sampler2D mask;
uniform bool enabled = true;

void fragment() {
    COLOR = texture(TEXTURE, UV) * tint;
    COLOR.rgb = mix(COLOR.rgb, vec3(1.0), flash);
}
```

Every uniform appears in the Inspector under the ShaderMaterial, with a widget decided by its hint. `: source_color` on a colour is not optional — without it Godot does no sRGB conversion and your colours are wrong.

From code:

```gdscript
var mat: ShaderMaterial = sprite.material
mat.set_shader_parameter("flash", 1.0)
var t := create_tween()
t.tween_method(func(v): mat.set_shader_parameter("flash", v), 1.0, 0.0, 0.2)
```

That is a hit flash in four lines, and it tweens.

**The sharing trap, again.** A `ShaderMaterial` assigned in the editor is one resource shared by every instance of that scene — setting `flash` on one enemy flashes all of them. Either `sprite.material = sprite.material.duplicate()` in `_ready()`, or use the per-instance route: `sprite.set_instance_shader_parameter("flash", 1.0)` with the uniform declared as `instance uniform float flash;`. The instance version is cheaper and does not break batching.

`shader_parameter/<name>` is the property path, so an `AnimationPlayer` can keyframe a uniform directly.
